  "overview": {
    "text": "Helen Adams Keller (1880–1968) was born in Tuscumbia, Alabama, and became deafblind after an illness at 19 months; the illness remains unidentified. In 1887, teacher Anne Sullivan began teaching her by spelling words into her hand, opening a path to communication and formal education. Keller attended Radcliffe College and graduated in 1904, the first deafblind person to earn a college degree. She published The Story of My Life in 1903, wrote many other works, and became a prominent advocate for people with disabilities, women’s suffrage, and workers’ rights. Her significance lies in both her literary career and decades of public advocacy for greater opportunity and access.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.afb.org/about-afb/history/helen-keller/biography-and-chronology/biography",
      "https://www.perkins.org/resource/helen-keller-timeline/"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "On April 5, 1887, Sullivan fingerspelled “W-A-T-E-R” into Keller’s hand as water flowed over it; Keller connected the word with what she felt.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.perkins.org/resource/helen-keller-timeline/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Keller graduated cum laude from Radcliffe in 1904, becoming the first deafblind person to earn a college degree.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.afb.org/about-afb/history/helen-keller/biography-and-chronology/biography",
        "https://www.perkins.org/resource/helen-keller-timeline/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Her autobiography, The Story of My Life, was published in 1903, while she was a student at Radcliffe.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.afb.org/about-afb/history/helen-keller/biography-and-chronology/biography",
        "https://www.perkins.org/resource/helen-keller-timeline/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Keller was a committed socialist, supported women’s suffrage, and became an early member of the American Civil Liberties Union.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.afb.org/about-afb/history/helen-keller/biography-and-chronology/biography"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "From 1924, she worked as a public spokesperson for the American Foundation for the Blind, advocating for people with vision loss.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.perkins.org/resource/helen-keller-timeline/",
        "https://www.afb.org/about-afb/history/helen-keller/biography-and-chronology/biography"
      ]
    }
  ],
